{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>וִידֻעִים לְשִׁבְטֵיכֶם</b> — \"Known to you: if someone comes before me wrapped in his tallit, I do not know who he is or from which tribe he is, but you recognize him, for you raised him.\" This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 1:13), from Sifrei. If so, <b>לְשִׁבְטֵיכֶם</b> is conjoined with <b>וִידוּעִים</b>. But according to the plain sense, the meaning is: Provide for yourselves, from your tribes, men who are wise. And in my opinion, the meaning of <b>וִידוּעִים</b> is that they are known to the judges — that is, their excellence is known and recognized among those who appoint them as judges. And he subsumed all the qualities of the judges under the word <b>וִידוּעִים</b>, for judges must be (Exodus 18:21): \"men of valor, God-fearing men, men of truth, haters of unjust gain,\" as Jethro said, and these qualities were known to the judges beforehand, for everyone would say, \"This one is fit to be a judge.\""], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "In the quotation from Exodus 18:21, the Hebrew reads אֱלֹהִים as אֱלֹהִים; rendered here as 'God-fearing' per standard English idiom for this phrase, though the divine name is preserved in the Hebrew quoted."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:43:51.407772962Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}